    "body": "Closes #125.\n\nImplements ADR-015: text now wraps within its container, whitespace collapses, and line layout is direction-aware (Arabic wraps correctly as RTL). Four commits, ~640 lines total.\n\n## The problem this fixes\n\nBefore this PR, every text node was laid out as a single line. A paragraph longer than its container overflowed past the viewport edge and was clipped. Firefox renders the same paragraph in multiple lines within its box. That alone made many real HTML files unreadable in HoneyBee.\n\nSeparately, whitespace did not collapse. HTML's default `white-space: normal` collapses runs of spaces and newlines into one space, but HoneyBee rendered them verbatim.\n\n## The decision (from ADR-015, already merged)\n\nThree coordinated changes:\n\n1. Whitespace collapsing per CSS `white-space: normal`.\n2. Line breaking at word boundaries, greedy fill.\n3. Direction-aware: first-strong heuristic detects RTL (Arabic, Hebrew, Syriac, Thaana, NKo, Adlam) vs LTR.\n\nDirection awareness is in v1 because naive LTR-only wrapping would regress Arabic rendering: words would be placed left-to-right within each line, scrambling reading order. Given HoneyBee's mission is \"Fast websites for Egypt,\" that regression is not acceptable.\n\n## Four commits\n\n**Commit 1** (`7c4ee4c`) -- whitespace collapsing + direction detection\n- New `text_utils` module: `collapse_whitespace`, `detect_rtl`\n- Layout's text-node branch calls `collapse_whitespace`; empty result skips the node\n- Visible change: source-indented HTML no longer renders irregular spacing\n\n**Commit 2** (`7245b34`) -- line breaking, pure internal refactor\n- `LineFragment` struct and `LayoutText::lines` vector\n- `break_into_lines` in `text_utils`\n- `TextMeasurer` signature changes from `(LayoutText)` to `(text, font_family, font_size)`\n- Layout assigns fragment x based on `rtl`: LTR at `content_x`, RTL at `content_x + content_w - width`\n- No visible change (paint still used whole text)\n- Verified against post-ADR-014 welcome page screenshot: identical\n\n**Commit 3** (`ad96894`) -- paint multi-line\n- `TextDrawer` gains `LineFragment` parameter\n- `paint.cpp` iterates `lt.lines`, draws per fragment\n- `pango_text.cpp`: `draw_line_with_pango(cairo_t*, LineFragment, LayoutText)`\n- `docs/wrap-test.html`: demonstration page (long English and Arabic paragraphs in 300px gray boxes)\n\n**Commit 4** (`8fc5901`) -- docs: note landing + document tradeoff\n- `docs/current-state.md`: wrapping moved to \"renders\", plus a \"Known behavior\" section on the RTL heuristic\n- `README.md`: feature matrix row added\n- `docs/decisions/README.md`: ADR-015 indexed\n\n## Visual verification\n\nLoaded `docs/wrap-test.html` in HoneyBee:\n\n- English paragraph: wraps across 5 lines, all left-aligned, within the 300px gray container\n- Arabic paragraph: wraps across 5 lines, each line flush-right against the container's right edge\n- Arabic letterforms connected through the fragment path (Pango shaping works)\n- Word order correct; sentence-final period appears on the left of the last line, as expected for RTL\n\nWelcome page after Commit 3 renders with one visible change: the fourth line (mixed Arabic + English) is now right-aligned, because the first strong character is Arabic.\n\n## Known behavior: RTL heuristic divergence\n\nThis PR accepts one visible behavior change that diverges from Firefox:\n\nFirefox determines paragraph direction from the inherited `direction` property (default LTR). A paragraph with Arabic text but no explicit `dir=\"rtl\"` renders LTR, so Arabic segments shape correctly but the paragraph starts at the left edge.\n\nHoneyBee applies a per-text-node first-strong heuristic. A paragraph whose first strong character is Arabic renders RTL (lines right-aligned), regardless of inherited direction.\n\nThe consequence on the welcome page: a mixed-direction paragraph (Arabic first, then English) renders right-aligned in HoneyBee but left-aligned in Firefox.\n\nThis is documented behavior, not a bug. Three options were considered:\n\n1. Apply heuristic unconditionally (chosen) -- mixed paragraphs right-align (diverges from Firefox), but all-Arabic paragraphs right-align correctly. Better for the target audience of Arabic-language content.\n2. Remove the heuristic -- matches Firefox exactly, but all-Arabic paragraphs also left-align. Worse for Arabic-only pages.\n3. Refine the heuristic -- only right-align if text contains no LTR letters. Better than both, but no heuristic-based approach matches Firefox; only dir attribute support does.\n\nThe true fix is `dir` attribute / `direction` CSS property support, deferred to ADR-016+. Documented in `docs/current-state.md` under \"Known behavior: RTL direction heuristic\".\n\n## Tests\n\n- 190 cases, 755 assertions, all pass (was 165/669 on main)\n- `[layout][text_utils]` -> 16 cases\n- `[layout][text_wrapping]` -> 7 cases\n- `[paint]` -> 9 cases\n\n## Deferred (per ADR-015)\n\n- `display: inline` for span/em/a (ADR-016+)\n- Full UAX #9 bidi reordering\n- white-space pre variants\n- line-height property\n- text-align center/right/justify\n- Hyphenation, word-break, overflow-wrap\n- CJK character-level breaking\n- dir attribute / direction CSS property (would resolve the known behavior above)\n\n## Next\n\nWith wrapping landed, real documentation pages, blog posts, and Wikipedia articles finally render readably. The next structural step is inline formatting (ADR-016): span, em, a currently render as blocks. That is what turns a paragraph with inline emphasis from three stacked blocks into one flowing line. It is also the natural point to introduce dir attribute support and resolve the heuristic divergence documented above.\n",
